+# =============================================================================
+# CI — build, test, verify and smoke the application the way it actually runs.
+#
+# WHAT CHANGED FROM THE PREVIOUS WORKFLOW, AND WHY
+# ------------------------------------------------
+# 1. RUNTIME ALIGNMENT. Every job used PHP 8.3 and Node 20 while
+#    `composer.json` requires PHP ^8.4 (and composer.lock pins that platform)
+#    and `package.json` requires Node >=24 <25 with `.npmrc`
+#    `engine-strict=true`. `composer install` and `npm ci` therefore failed on
+#    the lock file before any test ran. CI now uses PHP 8.4 and the Node version
+#    from `.nvmrc`, and `scripts/ci/assert-runtime-alignment.sh` fails the build
+#    if the workflow, the lock file and the manifests ever disagree again.
+#
+# 2. LEAST PRIVILEGE. A top-level `permissions: contents: read` plus a
+#    `concurrency` group. The previous workflow granted whatever the repository
+#    default was to every job, including the ones that only read files.
+#
+# 3. PINNED ACTIONS. Every action is pinned to a full commit SHA. A tag is a
+#    mutable pointer; a compromised or retargeted action runs with this
+#    repository's token.
+#
+# 4. THE LONG INLINE SHELL IS NOW IN scripts/ci/. The previous file embedded
+#    ~350 lines of bash inside YAML, which cannot be run locally, cannot be
+#    shellchecked, and breaks on a stray quote during an unrelated edit.
+#
+# 5. REAL RUNTIME COVERAGE. A job with MySQL + Redis services proves the
+#    migration suite, the queue lanes, the scheduler and the BACKUP/RESTORE
+#    DRILL against the engine that ships, not only against SQLite.
+#
+# 6. ARTIFACTS. The Vite build is uploaded, so the manifest that was asserted is
+#    the manifest that is deployed. The container job builds the production
+#    image and proves it runs as a non-root user with a passing healthcheck.
+#
+# Every job is independently failing on purpose: a broken Blade template and a
+# broken image must not hide behind the same red cross.
